Run these examples from the checkout in the
[ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')). The default CPU backend
exposes multiple logical devices on one host, so you can try both examples
without accelerator hardware.

# [Map tensor dimensions to devices]($heading.id('map-tensor-dimensions-to-devices'))

A **physical mesh** describes the devices the runtime can address and how
they are connected. A **logical mesh** names the axes the model uses to
distribute work. For example, `.data` can split a batch across groups of
devices and `.model` can split a weight dimension within each group.
`platform.registerMesh` maps the named axes to the physical topology using
their requested sizes and communication preferences.

Apply the mapping to a tagged shape with `Shape.withPartitioning`. The
sharding demo uses these mappings for its dense layer.

| Tensor | Global shape | Placement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Input | `batch=16, feature=32` | Split `batch` over `.data`. Each device gets all features for its batch slice. |
| Weights | `feature=32, hidden=64` | Split `hidden` over `.model`. The same weight shards are available to each data group. |
| Bias | `hidden=64` | Split `hidden` over `.model` to match the weight shards. |
| Output | `batch=16, hidden=64` | Split `batch` over `.data` and `hidden` over `.model`. |

```zig
const input_shape = zml.Shape.init(.{ .batch = 16, .feature = 32 }, .f32)
    .withPartitioning(mesh, .{ .batch = .data, .feature = .replicated });
```

Here `mesh` is the registered device mesh. A replicated dimension stays
whole on each shard that holds it. Replication therefore preserves the full
feature dimension while the batch can still be partitioned. For a graph
value inside `forward`, `Tensor.withPartitioning` emits a sharding constraint.
See the [tagged-shape guide]($link.page('zml/concepts')) and the
[sharding example source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/sharding/main.zig#L204-L236).

# [Run the sharding demo]($heading.id('run-the-sharding-demo'))

Start with automatic topology discovery. The default partitioner at this
revision is Shardy. The program also accepts `--partitioner=gspmd` to select
the GSPMD partitioner.

```sh
bazel run //examples/sharding -- --mesh=auto
```

Run on CPU with a mock topology to see data and model partitions on a
simulated eight-device grid.

```sh
bazel run //examples/sharding -- --mesh=mock --partitioner=shardy
```

Compare the physical topology, logical coordinates, and placement of the
input, weights, and bias in the logs. The demo applies a dense layer followed
by a per-shard computation and prints `output preview` with up to 16 values.
The discovered topology determines the actual split sizes.

# [Run data-parallel MNIST]($heading.id('run-data-parallel-mnist'))

```sh
bazel run //examples/mnist:mnist_dp
```

Bazel supplies the pretrained weights and test images. This variant fixes
`.data` to two groups and lets ZML infer the size of `.model`. It splits a
batch of four images so each data group receives two. When `.model` has
more than one device per group, the first layer is partitioned along its
output dimension and the second along its input dimension. The second
layer's bias is replicated.

Look for the printed mesh, a weight-transfer message, and four
`Shard … RECOGNIZED DIGIT` entries. Each entry is labeled by its index in
the batch. If you enable an accelerator backend, the available device
topology must support a data axis of size two. Use the CPU commands above
to try the examples on a machine with a single accelerator.
See [Bazel backend flags]($link.page('zml/build-and-run').ref('choose-a-backend-and-target-platform'))
and the [MNIST source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/mnist/mnist_data_parallel.zig).

The commands and expected output follow the pinned source. Device execution
checks are pending.
